{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.40,
      "why": "Pipelined gives one result per cycle with the shortest latency (N+2) and highest Fmax, which is the safest way to satisfy the 0.44 us p99 batch deadline for 32 simultaneous requests. Its area is higher due to a register per micro-rotation, so we search it to anchor the low-latency end of the Pareto front. The ranges target just enough accuracy (>=12 bits) while allowing guard bits and rounding to trade area for error."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.60,
      "why": "Pipelined_m registers every m stages, reducing FF count and thus luts_plus_ffs, which is the primary objective. It still produces one result per cycle, but latency grows to ceil(N/m)+2 and Fmax may drop with larger m. We search m=2..8 to map the area/latency/Fmax trade-off. The extra parameter m makes this family richer, so it receives the larger budget share. The other ranges match pipelined to keep accuracy comparable and isolate the effect of m."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires max_abs_err <= 2^-12, so data_width >= 14 and n_iter >= 12 are the minimum plausible values; we extend slightly upward to allow guard bits and rounding to meet the error bound with margin. Throughput >= 32 msps is easily met by both families at any realistic Fmax, but the system constraint sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 for 32 simultaneous requests is the critical one: it demands low latency and high Fmax. Pipelined provides the lowest latency and highest Fmax, while pipelined_m can reduce area at the cost of latency and Fmax. We therefore explore both to cover the Pareto front from low-area/longer-latency to high-area/low-latency. The budget split (40% pipelined, 60% pipelined_m) reflects that pipelined_m has an extra dimension (m) and is more likely to yield the area-minimal designs, while pipelined is needed to ensure the system deadline is met. All ranges are within the registry and focus on the feasible accuracy region to avoid wasting evaluations on designs that cannot meet the error constraint."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Final round and budget-limited: 60/100 evals used but round cap reached, so the run ends here. Infeasibility is ruled out by evidence — all three constraints are satisfiable simultaneously: throughput_msps best seen 273 vs >=32 (0% violations), max_abs_err best 5.95e-05 (2^-14.04) vs <=2^-12, and sys_p99_batch_us best 0.161 us vs <=0.44 us, with 20 feasible designs (16 unique). A non-empty feasible Pareto front exists. The front already spans both objectives well beyond the spec's requirements: luts_plus_ffs 1201..1773 against HV ref 4000 and accuracy_bits 12.6..14 against HV ref 12 (best accuracy 14.04 bits, 2 full bits better than the required 2^-12). The selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) picks pipelined_m [data_width=18 n_iter=14 angle_guard=1 frac_guard=1 rounding=round m=4] at luts_plus_ffs=1201, accuracy_bits=12.6, throughput 93.6 MSPS — comfortably inside all three constraints, and its sys_p99_batch margin (bound 0.44 us vs best-seen 0.161 us) gives headroom for the L2 system re-simulation. No family remains unexplored and cycle counts/throughput are already far above the 32 MSPS requirement, so widening or adding families would not change the selected design; further refinement would only chase HV coverage at the cost of a design the selection rule does not take."
}
